U({id:'gm4-1',track:'gm',level:4,title:'Number and algebra problems',icon:'∴',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Quadratic inequalities, simultaneous equations with a quadratic, bounds in calculations, rationalising harder surds and reverse percentages.',lessons:[
LH('gm4-1-1','Harder algebra',11,'Quadratic inequalities and solving a linear and a quadratic equation together.',[
T(`<h3>Quadratic inequalities</h3><ol><li>Solve the equation = 0 to find the roots.</li><li><b>Sketch</b> the U-shaped graph through the roots.</li><li>&lt; 0 means below the axis: <b>between</b> the roots. &gt; 0 means above: <b>outside</b> the roots.</li></ol>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_quadineq',need:4},`Quadratic inequalities. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Always sketch: between for < 0, outside for > 0.`),
T(`<h3>Linear and quadratic together</h3><p>Substitute the linear equation into the quadratic (or set the two y's equal), rearrange to = 0, and solve. Then find each y from the linear equation. There are usually <b>two</b> pairs of answers.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_simulquad',need:4},`Line meets curve. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Set equal, rearrange to 0, factorise.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Quadratic inequality: roots, sketch, then between or outside.</li><li>Line and curve: substitute, = 0, solve, find both y values.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm4-1-2','Bounds, surds and percentages',11,'Bounds in calculations, rationalising with conjugates, and reverse percentage problems.',[
RM(`<p>Upper bound of a ÷ b = upper a ÷ lower b. To rationalise 1/√a, multiply by √a/√a.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_bounds2',need:4},`Bounds in calculations. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Biggest top, smallest bottom for an upper bound.`),
T(`<h3>Rationalising with a conjugate</h3><p>For a denominator like 3 + √5, multiply top and bottom by <b>3 − √5</b>. The bottom becomes 3² − (√5)² = 9 − 5 = 4: no surd left.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_surdrat',need:4},`Rationalise the denominator. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Change the sign in the middle.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_reversepct',need:4},`Reverse percentages and compound interest. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Divide by the multiplier.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Bounds: think about which combination makes it biggest or smallest.</li><li>(a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b.</li><li>Reverse percentage: ÷ multiplier.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm4-2',track:'gm',level:4,title:'Geometry problems',icon:'⬚',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'3D Pythagoras and trigonometry, segments, and multi-step area, volume and similarity problems.',lessons:[
LH('gm4-2-1','3D Pythagoras and trigonometry',11,'Diagonals of cuboids and angles between a line and a plane.',[
T(`<h3>3D Pythagoras</h3><p>The longest diagonal of a cuboid: <b>d = √(a² + b² + c²)</b>. You can also do it in two steps: find the base diagonal first, then use it with the height.</p><h3>Angle with a plane</h3><p>Find the right-angled triangle that contains the line and its "shadow" on the base. Use trig in that triangle.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_pythag3d',need:5},`3D Pythagoras and angles. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Base diagonal first.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>d = √(a² + b² + c²).</li><li>Angle with the base: tan θ = height ÷ base diagonal.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm4-2-2','Area, volume and similarity problems',11,'Segments, composite volumes and similar solids in context.',[
RM(`<p>Sector = (θ/360)πr². Triangle = ½ab sin C. Area factor k², volume factor k³.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Segments</h3><div class="eq">segment = sector − triangle</div><p>Use ½r² sin θ for the triangle.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_segment',need:3},`Segment areas. Get 3 right to master it.`,`Sector minus triangle.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_simarea','g_volume','g_circle','g_areasine'],need:6},`Mixed area, volume and similarity. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Draw a sketch and label what you know.`),
Q(`A model is 1 : 50. The real car's volume is 8.75 m³. What is the model's volume?`,[`8.75 ÷ 125 000 m³`,'8.75 ÷ 50 m³',`8.75 ÷ 2500 m³`,'8.75 × 125 000 m³'],0,`Volume factor = 50³ = 125 000.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Segment = sector − triangle.</li><li>Scale factors: lengths k, areas k², volumes k³.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm4-3',track:'gm',level:4,title:'Statistics and probability problems',icon:'⚄',color:'#D4537E',desc:'Harder probability, algebraic probability, and interpreting statistical diagrams.',lessons:[
LH('gm4-3-1','Harder probability',11,'Conditional probability from tables, trees and Venn diagrams, and probability with algebra.',[
T(`<h3>Probability with algebra</h3><p>Some questions give probabilities in terms of x. Use the fact that probabilities add to 1, or set up an equation from what you're told, then solve.</p><p>Example: a bag has n counters, 3 red. Two are taken without replacement. P(both red) = 1/15. So 3/n × 2/(n − 1) = 1/15 → 6 × 15 = n(n − 1) → n² − n − 90 = 0 → n = 10.</p>`),
Q(`P(A) = 2x, P(B) = 3x, P(C) = x + 0.1. A, B and C are the only outcomes. Find x.`,[`0.15`,'0.1','0.2','0.25'],0,`6x + 0.1 = 1, so x = 0.15.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_condprob','g_venn'],need:6},`Conditional probability. Get 6 right to master it.`,`"Given that" restricts which group you look at.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Probabilities sum to 1: use it to form equations.</li><li>Without replacement: n then n − 1.</li><li>Given that: only count inside that group.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm4-3-2','Interpreting data',11,'Getting full marks on histograms, box plots and comparisons.',[
RM(`<p>Histogram area = frequency. Compare medians and IQRs, in context.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_hist','g_groupmean','g_median'],need:6},`Statistics calculations. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Frequency density × width = frequency.`),
Q(`Why might the estimated mean from grouped data be different from the true mean?`,[`It uses midpoints, not the actual values`,'It uses a different formula from the real mean','It leaves out the highest group of values','It rounds every value down to the nearest group'],0,`Classic 1-mark question.`),
Q(`Two classes' test scores: A has median 62, IQR 8; B has median 62, IQR 20. What's the best comparison?`,[`Same average, but A's scores were more consistent`,'A did better, because its IQR is smaller','B did better, because its scores spread higher','They did the same in every way: the medians match'],0,`Medians equal; A has less spread.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Grouped mean: an estimate because it uses midpoints.</li><li>Always compare an average and a spread, in context.</li></ul>`)])
]});
